## Clean Proof

**Theorem.** Let $X$ be a Hausdorff space determined by an upward filtered family $\{K_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A}$ of compact subsets. Let $Y$ be a metric space. Then the topology of uniform convergence on $\{K_\alpha\}$ on $C(X, Y)$ is weaker than (i.e., coarser than) the topology of uniform convergence on all compact subsets of $X$.

**Proof.**

Denote by $\mathcal{K}$ the family of all compact subsets of $X$. The topology of uniform convergence on a family $\mathcal{S}$ of subsets of $X$ is generated by basic neighborhoods of the form
$$N(f, S, \varepsilon) = \left\{g \in C(X,Y) : \sup_{x \in S} d(f(x), g(x)) < \varepsilon\right\}, \qquad f \in C(X,Y),\ S \in \mathcal{S},\ \varepsilon > 0.$$

Since each $K_\alpha$ is a compact subset of $X$ by hypothesis, we have $\{K_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A} \subseteq \mathcal{K}$. Therefore, every basic neighborhood $N(f, K_\alpha, \varepsilon)$ in the topology of uniform convergence on $\{K_\alpha\}$ is also a basic neighborhood in the topology of uniform convergence on $\mathcal{K}$. Consequently, every open set in the $\{K_\alpha\}$-topology is open in the $\mathcal{K}$-topology, i.e.,
$$\tau_{\{K_\alpha\}} \subseteq \tau_{\mathcal{K}}. \qquad \blacksquare$$

**Remark (equality for $k_\omega$-spaces).** When the determining family is a countable increasing sequence $\{K_n\}_{n=1}^\infty$ (i.e., $X$ is a $k_\omega$-space), the two topologies are in fact **equal**. This follows from the classical result that in a $k_\omega$-space, every compact subset $C$ of $X$ is contained in some $K_n$. The proof: if $C \not\subseteq K_n$ for any $n$, choose $x_n \in C \setminus K_n$. Then $S = \{x_n\}$ is an infinite closed discrete subspace of $X$ (since $S \cap K_m$ is finite for each $m$, hence closed in $K_m$, and $X$ carries the inductive limit topology). But $S \subseteq C$ with $C$ compact forces $S$ to be compact, hence finite — contradiction. Given $C \subseteq K_n$, we have $\sup_{x \in C} d(f,g) \leq \sup_{x \in K_n} d(f,g)$, so every $\mathcal{K}$-neighborhood contains a $\{K_n\}$-neighborhood, giving $\tau_{\mathcal{K}} \subseteq \tau_{\{K_n\}}$ and thus equality.

